by nicolas-grekas at 2026-07-27T03:34:06Z

Reviewed, rebased onto the current 8.2 and reworked. Thanks @Tiriel, and sorry for the very long wait.

The config node was only half the problem. As submitted, the option compiled into the container and did nothing at runtime for scoped clients, which is the only place it could be set. `ScopingHttpClient::request()` resolves the URL against the scope base URI and then `unset($options["base_uri"])`, so the retryable client, sitting below it at priority 25, received an absolute URL and no base URI. `shiftBaseUri()` had nothing to act on and every attempt went to the same host.

I could not see this from the tests because they assert on the service definition: the class, `getArgument(2)` and the method call. Those pass whether or not the feature works. A functional test recording the URLs that reach the transport shows it plainly:

```
-    1 => "GET https://b.example.com/posts/1"
+    1 => "GET https://a.example.com/posts/1"
```

So the retryable client is now decorated at priority 12 instead of 25 when `base_uris` is set, which puts it outside the scoping client. It sees the relative URL, injects a string base URI per attempt, and scoping resolves it as usual. The reorder is conditional on purpose: it exists only to serve this feature, so nobody else should pay for it. I dumped and diffed a container built from the existing `http_client_retry` fixture before and after, and it is byte identical.

@nicolas-grekas, the caching question you would have hit: at 12 the retryable client is also outside `CachingHttpClient` at 20, so retries re-enter the cache. I think that is fine and slightly better. Retries only happen after a failure, failures are not cached, and a rotated URI has a different cache key, so a retry misses and reaches the transport just as before. A cache hit also stops paying for the `AsyncResponse` retry machinery. It is noted in the PR body.

Your strings-only request from December 2023, restated by @javiereguiluz, is applied: the prototype is a `stringPrototype()` that cannot be empty. I dropped the `fixXmlConfig` singular alias, since XML bundle configuration no longer exists on 8.2 and it only reintroduced the `base_uri` / `base_uris` confusion.

Three configurations that used to be accepted silently are now rejected: `base_uris` without a `scope`, which is your point about the scope regexp no longer matching once the host rotates; `base_uris` with retries disabled; and `base_uris` with a single entry, which `shiftBaseUri()` degrades to a plain base URI.

Also fixed: the default client never received the list. Only the scoped call site passed it, so `default_options.retry_failed.base_uris` validated, compiled to `withOptions(["base_uri" => []])`, and did nothing, in the one place where the mechanism would have worked without any reordering.

Finally I dropped three changes that were unrelated to the goal and changed the container for existing setups: the unconditional `withOptions` call, which made every retryable client clone its whole inner chain for an empty array; the `http_client.client` tag on the scoping service, which made the profiler list those clients twice; and the `array_filter` replacing `unset($scopeConfig["base_uri"])`, which leaked a `base_uri` default option into `CachingHttpClient`.

