by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-14T07:43:41Z

Rebased onto the current 8.2 and re-reviewed. Thanks for your patience, @Tiriel.

Two things I checked rather than assumed, since both looked risky:

**The scope alternation is safe.** `forBaseUris()` joins `preg_quote()`d URIs with `|`, and the match is `preg_match("{{$regexp}}A", $url)`. The `A` modifier anchors every alternative, not just the first, so a crafted URL cannot widen the scope:

```
https://a.example.com/x                  => 1
https://b.example.com/x                  => 1
https://evil.com/https://b.example.com/x => 0
```

**The priority drop from 25 to 12 does what its comment says.** `DecoratorServicePass` feeds an `SplPriorityQueue`, which yields the highest priority first, so the highest priority is the innermost decorator. 12 therefore places `RetryableHttpClient` outside `ScopingHttpClient` (15), which is what makes the rotated base URI survive scoping.

One consequence that was not written down: 12 also places it outside `CachingHttpClient` (20), so for clients using `base_uris`, retries now wrap the cache rather than sitting inside it, and a retried request can be served from cache. That looks right to me, retrying the whole cached operation rather than only the transport, but it is a behaviour change beyond scoping and worth being explicit about. I extended the decorator-ordering comment in `FrameworkExtension` to record the conditional priority.

Verified: `ScopingHttpClientTest` and the new functional `HttpClientTest` green at 16, FrameworkBundle DI green at 578, PHPStan clean on `ScopingHttpClient`. Restricting the alternation to the first URI fails two of those tests, so the multi-URI scope is covered rather than incidental.

The changelog entries moved to the 8.2 sections of both packages after the rebase.
